Wine's auto-generated winevulkan thunks (dlls/winevulkan/loader_thunks.c)
assert on any non-VK_SUCCESS return from vkGetPastPresentationTimingEXT and
vkGetRefreshCycleDurationGOOGLE. The gamescope WSI layer returns
VK_ERROR_SURFACE_LOST_KHR for any swapchain it didn't create itself — but
modern frame-generation wrappers (NVIDIA Streamline / DLSS-FG) interpose
between the application and the layer's CreateSwapchainKHR, so the
"effective" swapchain the application receives is opaque to the layer.

When the application later polls GetPastPresentationTimingGOOGLE on its
(Streamline-wrapped) swapchain, the layer's lookup fails, it returns
VK_ERROR_SURFACE_LOST_KHR, Wine asserts, the game crashes with an
"Assertion failed!" dialog. Symptom in our case: PRAGMATA reaches its
menu (we hear the audio) then aborts.

Fix: return VK_SUCCESS with zero past timings instead, which matches the
spec's "no timing data available yet" interpretation rather than a fatal
error. For GetRefreshCycleDurationGOOGLE, fall back to 60Hz (16.67ms in
nanoseconds) — we have no way to query the real refresh from this code
path without a tracked swapchain, but 60Hz is the safest assumption.

Upstream context: ValveSoftware/gamescope#1592, #1384. The Wine assertion
itself is arguably a Wine bug (the Vulkan spec allows these functions to
fail), but fixing that requires teaching dlls/winevulkan/make_vulkan to
handle this extension's failure modes — out of scope.

--- a/layer/VkLayer_FROG_gamescope_wsi.cpp
+++ b/layer/VkLayer_FROG_gamescope_wsi.cpp
@@ -1502,8 +1502,15 @@
             VkPastPresentationTimingGOOGLE* pPresentationTimings) {
       auto gamescopeSwapchain = GamescopeSwapchain::get(swapchain);
       if (!gamescopeSwapchain) {
-        fprintf(stderr, "[Gamescope WSI] GetPastPresentationTimingGOOGLE: Not a gamescope swapchain.\n");
-        return VK_ERROR_SURFACE_LOST_KHR;
+        // Don't return VK_ERROR_SURFACE_LOST_KHR for swapchains not tracked
+        // by this layer — Wine's winevulkan thunk asserts on non-VK_SUCCESS
+        // and crashes the game. Streamline/DLSS-FG wraps swapchain creation
+        // in a way that hides the real VkSwapchainKHR from the layer, so
+        // this fires for any game using those. Return VK_SUCCESS with zero
+        // timings, matching the spec's "no data yet" interpretation.
+        if (pPresentationTimingCount)
+          *pPresentationTimingCount = 0;
+        return VK_SUCCESS;
       }
 
       // Dispatch to get the latest timings.
@@ -1529,8 +1536,13 @@
             VkRefreshCycleDurationGOOGLE*   pDisplayTimingProperties) {
       auto gamescopeSwapchain = GamescopeSwapchain::get(swapchain);
       if (!gamescopeSwapchain) {
-        fprintf(stderr, "[Gamescope WSI] GetRefreshCycleDurationGOOGLE: Not a gamescope swapchain.\n");
-        return VK_ERROR_SURFACE_LOST_KHR;
+        // See GetPastPresentationTimingGOOGLE above — same Wine-thunk
+        // assertion issue. Fall back to 60Hz (16.67ms in ns); we have no
+        // way to read the real refresh from this code path without a
+        // tracked swapchain, but 60Hz is the safest modern-display default.
+        if (pDisplayTimingProperties)
+          pDisplayTimingProperties->refreshDuration = 16666667;
+        return VK_SUCCESS;
       }
 
       // Dispatch to get the latest cycle.
